input.rule:
split_path(p) is a pure, byte-oriented split of the input string on the single ASCII byte '/' (0x2F), discarding every empty piece. Nothing else. No trimming, no decoding, no normalization, no case folding, no dedup, no dot-segment handling, no other separator.

Exact algorithm (implementable verbatim):

  fn split_path(p: &[u8]) -> Vec<Vec<u8>> {
      let mut out = Vec::new();
      let mut cur: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
      for &b in p {
          if b == b'/' {                 // 0x2F is the ONLY separator
              if !cur.is_empty() { out.push(std::mem::take(&mut cur)); }
              else { cur.clear(); }      // empty piece dropped
          } else {
              cur.push(b);
          }
      }
      if !cur.is_empty() { out.push(cur); }
      out
  }

Equivalently: `p.split('/').filter(|s| !s.is_empty()).map(String::from).collect()`.

Fully pinned properties, each observed:

1. Separator set = exactly {'/'}. A sweep of every byte 1..=255 placed (a) inside a segment (`/aXb`) and (b) as a lone segment (`/X/z`) changes the segment count for no byte except 0x2F. Bytes 0x23 ('#') and 0x3F ('?') change the count only because URL::parse strips the fragment/query BEFORE split_path is reached — split_path itself never sees them. Backslash '\\' (0x5C) is an ordinary character: `/\\/\\\\` -> 2 segments ["\\", "\\\\"]. (Byte 0x00 is untestable through argv.)

2. NO trimming whatsoever, despite the existence of an internal trim(). Segments keep leading/trailing/interior whitespace byte-for-byte, and whitespace-only segments SURVIVE and are counted:
   - `/ /`      -> 1 segment: " "
   - `/a /`     -> 1: "a "
   - `/ a/`     -> 1: " a"
   - `/  a  /`  -> 1: "  a  "
   - `/   `     -> 1: "   "
   - `/ / / `   -> 3: " ", " ", " "
   - `/a/ /b`   -> 3: "a", " ", "b"
   - `/ a / b /`-> 2: " a ", " b " (verified with od -c)
   - `/\t/\x0b/\r/\x0c/ /\xff` -> 6 segments, each the single byte, verified with od -c.
   Therefore there is no "drop if empty after trim" step; only literally-empty pieces are dropped.

3. NO url_decode anywhere in this pipeline. "%41" stays "%41" (does not become "A"); "%2E%2E" stays "%2E%2E"; "%20" stays "%20"; "+" stays "+"; and critically "%2F" is NOT a separator: `/a%2Fb` -> 1 segment "a%2Fb", `/%2F` -> 1 segment "%2F".

4. NO dot-segment removal, NO dedup: `/./ /..` -> 3 [".", " ", ".."]; `/a/a/a/a` -> 4 ["a","a","a","a"].

5. Empty / all-separator inputs give 0 segments (LINE 5 prints "0", no LINE 6+): path "" -> 0; "/" -> 0; "///" -> 0; 1000 consecutive '/' -> 0.

6. Leading and trailing '/' contribute nothing; runs of '/' collapse. Relative (no leading '/') works identically: "a/b/c" -> 3; "/a/b/c" -> 3; "//a/b" -> 2; "a//b" -> 2; "/a//" -> 1.

7. Byte transparency: all high bytes 0x80..0xFF are preserved verbatim, no UTF-8 awareness, no validation. `/\xc3\xa9\xff\x80/\xe4\xb8\xad` -> 2 segments with exactly those bytes (od -c confirmed). A 10 000-byte segment of repeated "\xc3\xa9" round-trips with an identical md5.

8. Order is input order (first segment printed first). Count is printed as a plain decimal integer with no locale grouping: 1000 -> "1000", 9999/10000/20000/30000 -> "9999"/"10000"/"20000"/"30000". A single 100 000-byte segment yields count 1 and the segment is emitted in full (100 001 bytes with newline). No length caps or truncation observed at any size (only the OS argv limit stops larger probes).

9. What split_path sees is exactly URL::path(): query (from the first '?') and fragment (from the first '#') are removed by parse before split_path, and no whole-string trim happens either — `'  http://e.com/a  '` gives path "/a  " -> 1 segment "a  " (and scheme "  http" is invalid, host "e.com" valid).

